// The same rule for the `if` of a guard (spec/01-lexical.md, row
// `suffix-boundary`). Before the suffix existed, `case 1if t` was the pattern
// `1` with the guard `if t`, a valid arm that matched. Now it is the Int
// literal `1i` and the name `f`, a syntax error. A consumer that has no Int
// literal yet runs the program, so this probe fails today on every consumer
// and is on each expected-failure list.
//
// A static error rejects the program: a non-zero exit status, and nothing
// runs, so nothing prints (spec/README.md, Conformance). The first line
// prints `ran`, so an implementation that finds the error only when it runs
// the program prints `ran` and fails this probe.

print "ran";
var t = true;
fun pick(x) {
    return match x {
        case 1if t => "guarded"
        case _ => "other"
    };
}
print pick(1);

// STATIC-ERROR
// STATUS: error
// COVERS: literal suffix-boundary
